{% block title %}The Waasmoowin deal, in the public record | RHT Circle{% endblock %}
{% block description %}Eight First Nations can collectively invest in up to half of two Hydro One transmission lines. The opportunity is public. Sagamok's ownership, borrowing, cost-sharing and procurement terms are not.{% endblock %}
{% block og_title %}The Waasmoowin deal, in the public record{% endblock %}
{% block og_description %}The Waasmoowin opportunity is public. Sagamok's ownership, borrowing, cost-sharing and procurement terms are not.{% endblock %}

{% block article_body %}
  <p class="lead">Waasmoowin puts eight Robinson Huron Treaty First Nations in a position to own up to half of the transmission-line parts of two Hydro One projects between Sault Ste. Marie and Greater Sudbury.</p>

  <p>That opportunity is real. So are the jobs, contracting, training and land-protection work described in the public project material.</p>

  <p>The terms for Sagamok are not public. Members cannot yet see what the Nation may borrow, what it may have to pay, how cost increases are divided, how contracts are allocated or how the return will reach the community.</p>

  <section class="news-article__questions">
    <h2>Six records would show the deal</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>The signed Cooperation and Support Agreement with Hydro One.</li>
      <li>The amended Multi-Project Capacity Funding Agreement.</li>
      <li>The partnership agreements, ownership schedules and rules for Waasmoowin GP Inc., Waasmoowin NSL LP and Waasmoowin NEPL LP.</li>
      <li>The amount Sagamok may borrow or contribute, the security for that money and the rule for dividing cost increases.</li>
      <li>The procurement rules showing how jobs and contracts are divided among the eight Nations.</li>
      <li>The environmental commitments and the record showing how Sagamok's land, water, medicines, hunting and herbicide concerns were answered.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <h2>What the public structure says</h2>

  <p>The North Shore Link is planned as a 230 kV line from Sault Ste. Marie to the Mississagi Transformer Station near Wharncliffe. The Northeast Power Line is planned as a 500 kV line of about 205 kilometres from Greater Sudbury to Mississagi.</p>

  <p>Hydro One's model gives nearby First Nations the opportunity to invest in up to 50 per cent of the transmission-line part of each new large project. That does not mean Hydro One is giving away half the whole project. The First Nations must invest for the ownership stake, and Hydro One keeps the station assets.</p>

  <p>The eight partners are Atikameksheng Anishnawbek, Batchewana First Nation, Mississauga First Nation, Sagamok Anishnawbek, Serpent River First Nation, Thessalon First Nation, Wahnapitae First Nation and Whitefish River First Nation.</p>

  <p>The public Waasmoowin newsletter separates the work into two bodies. Waasmoowin Opportunities and Consultation Council handles community engagement, land and water protection, training and business opportunities. Waasmoowin GP Inc. handles ownership and financial benefits. Public reporting also identifies Waasmoowin NSL LP and Waasmoowin NEPL LP as the partnerships intended to hold the two ownership interests.</p>

  <p>That is enough to show the outline. It is not enough to show each Nation's share, voting rights, borrowing limit, cost responsibility or return.</p>

  <h2>Sagamok's approvals began in 2023</h2>

  <p>Sagamok Council minutes record the Hydro One work in stages.</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>June 22, 2023:</strong> Council approved a Hydro One memorandum of understanding and $20,000 for early project work.</li>
    <li><strong>July 27, 2023:</strong> Council accepted the 50-50 model while the First Nations were forming a limited partnership.</li>
    <li><strong>April 16, 2024:</strong> Council received a briefing that recorded proposed support from Hydro One, including $100,000 a year for an engagement coordinator for at least five years, $615,000 for engagement, field work and professional support, and $30,000 for a greenhouse project. The briefing said the ownership investment would not be required until the line was operating.</li>
    <li><strong>February 3, 2026:</strong> Council approved the Cooperation and Support Agreement and a new company for procurement work.</li>
    <li><strong>February 18, 2026:</strong> Council approved an amended capacity-funding agreement and directed Sagamok Development Corporation to carry out the business work.</li>
    <li><strong>February 24, 2026:</strong> Hydro One and Waasmoowin publicly signed their agreement.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>The public signing described consultation, procurement, training and Indigenous-led construction monitoring. It did not publish the agreement.</p>

  <h2>The development budgets rose by $67.2 million</h2>

  <p>On December 11, 2025, the Ontario Energy Board recorded two increases in Hydro One's development budgets.</p>

  <ul>
    <li>North Shore Link rose from <strong>$52.7 million to $82.3 million</strong>, an increase of 56.1 per cent.</li>
    <li>Northeast Power Line rose from <strong>$50.5 million to $88.1 million</strong>, an increase of 74.5 per cent.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>The two increases total $67.2 million.</p>

  <p>These are Hydro One development budgets. They are not Sagamok's bill, they are not the price of Sagamok's ownership stake, and they do not prove Sagamok has taken on any of the increase.</p>

  <p>The OEB asked Hydro One to explain the main causes, list the added studies and work, explain why the work was moved earlier, and confirm the total expected cost of both projects. Hydro One had said the added development work did not increase the total cost to deliver the projects.</p>

  <p>That distinction matters. The public record shows a large increase in early project spending. Only the signed financing and cost-sharing terms can show what that means for the eight First Nations.</p>

  <h2>Garden River left the group, not the project record</h2>

  <p>Garden River First Nation withdrew from Waasmoowin on June 10, 2025. The group went from nine Nations to eight.</p>

  <p>Hydro One's 2026 project material still identifies Garden River as a separate First Nation partner. The North Shore Link material describes nine project partners: Garden River plus the eight Waasmoowin Nations.</p>

  <p>The accurate statement is narrow. Garden River left the Waasmoowin consortium. The available Hydro One record does not show that it left the transmission projects.</p>

  <h2>The regulatory process is live</h2>

  <p>The Northeast Power Line is now before the Ontario Energy Board as case EB-2026-0154. The OEB is considering whether Hydro One may build the line and related facilities. Members can follow the case or submit a letter of comment.</p>

  <p>The OEB says its hearing does not decide environmental issues or the Crown's duty to consult unless they directly affect price, reliability or service quality. The environmental and treaty-rights record has to be followed separately.</p>

  <h2>What the record supports</h2>

  <p>Waasmoowin may produce ownership income, work, training, contracts and a stronger role in how the two lines are built. The public documents describe those goals.</p>

  <p>The same documents do not show what Sagamok must put in, what it may borrow, what it receives, who checks the results or what happens if costs rise again.</p>

  <p>This is not evidence that the deal is bad. It is evidence that the deal is material and that the signed terms matter.</p>

  <p>Members do not need another announcement. They need the agreements and a plain accounting of Sagamok's share.</p>
